00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:53,860
At that point, that's going to tell you if you can open this door or not.

251
00:14:54,220 --> 00:14:58,000
The front and rear exits also require 50 pounds of force to push open.

252
00:14:58,001 --> 00:15:01,830
Release seatbelts. Release seatbelts. Release seatbelts. Release seatbelts.

253
00:15:01,831 --> 00:15:03,720
Release seatbelts. Release seatbelts.

254
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:10,400
The flight attendant pulls a handle to activate the emergency slide.

255
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:14,080
A tank of compressed gas inflates it in a matter of seconds.

256
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:17,060
Come this way. This way.

257
00:15:17,061 --> 00:15:19,960
Emergency slides can accommodate 70 passengers per minute.

258
00:15:19,961 --> 00:15:21,660
Everything cross your arms, jump!

259
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:27,040
Jumping onto the slide instead of sitting down increases evacuation speed by 50%.

260
00:15:27,041 --> 00:15:33,380
In the event of a water landing, the emergency slides have a number of safety features.

261
00:15:34,360 --> 00:15:38,820
They're all battery-powered and lighted, so if it's dark, we can light the area and they can see.

262
00:15:38,940 --> 00:15:42,720
The slides are also fully detachable in the event that we happen to land in the water.

263
00:15:43,340 --> 00:15:46,300
In this case, the exit itself becomes a life raft.

264
00:15:47,060 --> 00:15:51,960
That's just what happened in January of 2009,

265
00:15:51,961 --> 00:15:54,560
when a U.S. Airways flight was forced to land

266
00:15:54,561 --> 00:15:56,760
in the Hudson River near New York City.

267
00:15:57,340 --> 00:15:59,980
Passengers and crew escaped with no life-threatening injuries.

268
00:16:00,980 --> 00:16:02,660
Some waited on the wings for rescue.

269
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:05,780
Others used the front emergency slides as life rafts.

270
00:16:07,220 --> 00:16:09,380
The emergency exits on the aircraft are the exits

271
00:16:09,381 --> 00:16:11,140
that we hope you never have to use.

272
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:13,700
But if you do need them, those exits can save your life.

273
00:16:13,701 --> 00:16:17,140
Of course, there's an even faster way to get out of a jet.

274
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:21,420
The ejection seat.

275
00:16:23,300 --> 00:16:26,180
If a military pilot loses control of his craft,

276
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:28,400
experiences a mechanical failure,

277
00:16:28,980 --> 00:16:31,160
or is involved in a mid-air collision,

278
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:33,580
he probably needs to exit fast.

279
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:46,540
All they're concerned about is pulling that handle.

280
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:48,700
Nothing else matters in that situation

281
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,440
except pulling that handle.

282
00:16:50,540 --> 00:16:52,180
You have to get out now.

283
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:56,140
I've had crew members say that he thought he passed out.

284
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:57,820
And I explained to him, I think you just blinked,

285
00:16:57,920 --> 00:16:59,320
is how fast it happened.

286
00:16:59,420 --> 00:17:00,720
I've had other crew members that

287
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:03,080
had such a heightened sense of awareness,

288
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:06,020
they saw everything in slow motion.

289
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:09,220
In an F-8 team, the pilot pulls the handle

290
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:12,460
and three gunpowder charges blow the canopy off.

291
00:17:12,461 --> 00:17:16,260
Four milliseconds later, our rocket fires behind the seat,

292
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,800
launching pilot and chair into the sky.

293
00:17:20,140 --> 00:17:22,980
Once he's out, he goes up about 400 feet,

294
00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:26,120
the standard safe distance away from the jet.

295
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:32,020
The ejection exposes him to 10 to 14 Gs.

296
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:35,180
The entire process takes only two seconds.

297
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:38,800
As dangerous as this might seem,

298
00:17:38,801 --> 00:17:43,500
This is safer than being in an automobile accident.

299
00:17:43,501 --> 00:17:46,480
If you have an accident with this system,

300
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:49,820
all you do is pull the handle and you're instantly out.

301
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,780
The earliest pilots had to climb out

302
00:17:53,781 --> 00:17:55,540
and activate their own parachutes.

303
00:17:56,080 --> 00:17:57,820
As planes reached higher speeds,

304
00:17:58,320 --> 00:17:59,680
such escapes grew impossible.

305
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:02,720
The first ejection seats used compressed air.

306
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:08,500
But explosive charges were introduced in the mid-1940s.

307
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:12,840
Most pilots eject at low altitudes and speeds,

308
00:18:12,841 --> 00:18:17,920
and one successfully ejected at Mach 3.25 at 80,000 feet.

309
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:24,520
At the Dryden Flight Research Center at Edwards Air Force Base,

310
00:18:25,220 --> 00:18:29,300
pilots are trained on unique simulators for a literal last-ditch situation.

311
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:34,640
Yes, we want them to get to the point where they don't have to think about what to do,

312
00:18:34,740 --> 00:18:36,300
they just do it from practice.

313
00:18:36,301 --> 00:18:41,160
Pilots practice ejecting by going through each step in slow motion.

314
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:48,120
In full flight gear, they pull the handles, watch the canopy rise, and feel the seat go up.

315
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:54,080
I think there's a definite value in using a trainer like this versus

316
00:18:54,081 --> 00:18:58,700
the live ejection seat in an airplane because you can pull the handles.

317
00:18:58,701 --> 00:18:59,760
Eject, eject.

318
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:05,880
You get to touch buttons that you wouldn't normally touch in a live seat.

319
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:11,300
Exiting by ejection seat at 15,000 feet may be extreme,

320
00:19:11,301 --> 00:19:20,300
but to exit even higher, you have to take a ride into space.

321
00:19:20,301 --> 00:19:23,300
Orbiting 200 miles above the Earth,

322
00:19:23,301 --> 00:19:25,300
astronauts at the International Space Station

323
00:19:25,301 --> 00:19:29,300
make spacewalks to perform experiments and conduct repairs.

324
00:19:29,301 --> 00:19:33,300
Top Gear 8, standing to work on 7.

325
00:19:33,301 --> 00:19:36,040
They use the Quest Joint Air Lock to exit.

326
00:19:36,740 --> 00:19:40,060
It consists of two cylindrical chambers connected by a hatch.

327
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:43,060
The astronauts sleep overnight in the equipment lock,

328
00:19:43,780 --> 00:19:47,020
where the pressure gradually drops to 30% of Earth's atmosphere.

329
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:51,540
After donning their suits, they enter the crew lock,

330
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:53,620
where the pressure goes to 20%.

331
00:19:53,621 --> 00:19:57,000
Once the remaining atmosphere vents through the outer hatch

332
00:19:57,001 --> 00:20:00,080
and the pressure inside equals the vacuum outside,

333
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:01,480
it's time to exit.

334
00:20:03,300 --> 00:20:07,300
You can't get further outside than that.

335
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:09,800
Conducting a successful spacewalk may involve

336
00:20:09,900 --> 00:20:12,800
the ultimate exit.

337
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:15,240
Exiting into space may be intimidating,

338
00:20:15,340 --> 00:20:17,740
but back on Earth, there are some daunting entries

339
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:19,140
and exits, too.

340
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:21,360
Ever wonder what happens if you get trapped inside

341
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:22,760
a revolving door?

342
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:24,360
Unauthorized entry.

343
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:29,300
Ejection seats have saved the lives

344
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:32,400
of over 12,000 pilots.

345
00:20:32,401 --> 00:20:38,240
Enter and Exit will return on Modern Marvels, here on History.

346
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:45,800
We now return to Enter and Exit on Modern Marvels.

347
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:53,420
We don't think much about the doors in our homes, but we enter and exit through them all day, every day.

348
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:59,440
The U.S. alone manufactures 56.5 million interior doors every year.

349
00:21:00,440 --> 00:21:06,260
An average American home has about 15 from bedroom to bathroom doors and closets.

350
00:21:06,840 --> 00:21:11,380
Add three to that to cover the outside, front, rear, and patio doors.

351
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:20,580
Doors are a great opportunity to put your own personality and type of design into your home.

352
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:25,360
You often take your own doors for granted, but it's something you notice when you go into somebody else's house.

353
00:21:25,980 --> 00:21:29,200
Whether they're mass-producing or crafting one-of-a-kinds,

354
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:32,560
Door manufacturers of all sizes have one thing in common.

355
00:21:34,400 --> 00:21:35,980
They make them by hand.

356
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:43,960
Rogue Valley Door produces both mass market and custom doors.

357
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:50,200
A unique door comes to life when a client creates a design through the use of their CAD program.

358
00:21:52,660 --> 00:21:56,400
25 to 30 board feet of wood go into the average residential door.

359
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:59,740
Most of that is made up of lower-quality wood.

360
00:22:01,900 --> 00:22:03,440
It may have knots in it.

361
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:07,260
It may have stain or color defect or mineral streaks,

362
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:08,760
which would be similar to knots.

363
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:14,280
These less-than-perfect pieces get finger-jointed into long boards.

364
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:18,280
We put glue in those and they're pressed together.

365
00:22:18,940 --> 00:22:23,240
We can make pretty much any kind of leaf we want for our operation by doing that.

366
00:22:24,180 --> 00:22:26,080
These boards are known as core.

367
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:28,320
and they make up the interior of a door.

368
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:32,280
The outer layers, front, back, and sides,

369
00:22:32,780 --> 00:22:34,460
are what people see when they approach.

370
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:38,480
So that part, the veneer, should be the best of the best.

371
00:22:39,860 --> 00:22:42,220
Naughty pine or Douglas fir becomes core,

372
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:45,860
while specialty woods like oak and maple provide veneer.

373
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:50,040
Before slicing strips of veneer,

374
00:22:50,041 --> 00:22:53,020
the top-quality wood has to get hot and wet.

375
00:22:53,021 --> 00:23:01,260
It is heated to approximately 170 degrees internal temperature to make it easier to slice.

376
00:23:01,440 --> 00:23:08,800
Dry wood or cold wood does not slice as readily as heated wood.

377
00:23:11,560 --> 00:23:17,080
Rogue Valley slices veneer anywhere from one-tenth to one-sixteenth of an inch thick.

378
00:23:17,081 --> 00:23:25,220
Once they glue strips of veneer onto core blanks, workers prep the parts for final assembly

379
00:23:25,221 --> 00:23:28,520
by cutting raised edges, or profiles, into them.

380
00:23:30,420 --> 00:23:34,660
The profile is a detail that's machined into the components, and you have a male and a female

381
00:23:34,661 --> 00:23:40,540
component that fits together, or mates together, as you build a door, and that's what holds

382
00:23:40,541 --> 00:23:42,960
the door together as well as hold all the panels in place.

383
00:23:42,961 --> 00:23:50,900
If a door will have panels, employees carve designs into them, from simple edging to complex

384
00:23:50,901 --> 00:23:51,900
patterns.

385
00:23:51,901 --> 00:23:59,300
At the final stage, workers hand assemble the doors using mallets.

386
00:23:59,301 --> 00:24:04,420
They put the elements together like a ladder, with one side, or style, first.

387
00:24:04,421 --> 00:24:08,160
Next come the crosswise parts, or rails, and the panels.

388
00:24:08,161 --> 00:24:11,580
Finally, they hammer the second style into place.

389
00:24:11,581 --> 00:24:23,644
There really is no machine capable of the type of flexibility necessary to assemble all the different types of doors and parts that go into doors, one right after

390
00:24:23,645 --> 00:24:24,345
another.

391
00:24:25,420 --> 00:24:33,560
We may not manufacture the largest, the biggest, or the strongest door, but the sheer volume of the different doors that we make makes us unique.

392
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:38,680
Away from your home, you're still surrounded by entries and exits.

393
00:24:38,681 --> 00:24:45,680
The Boone Edom Company manufactures revolving doors and turnstiles for commercial buildings.

394
00:24:45,681 --> 00:24:51,680
Well, the turnstiles were developed in the late 1800s, but they generally are credited

395
00:24:51,681 --> 00:24:58,680
with mass use by the Piggly Wiggly grocery chain, who started using them in the 1920s

396
00:24:58,681 --> 00:25:03,680
to control traffic of their customers in and out of their grocery stores.

397
00:25:03,681 --> 00:25:07,680
Turnstiles gained early acceptance, hurting people at

398
00:25:07,681 --> 00:25:09,680
stadiums and in subways.

399
00:25:09,681 --> 00:25:12,680
Tickets could be collected more easily, and you could get an

400
00:25:12,681 --> 00:25:14,680
accurate head count.

401
00:25:14,681 --> 00:25:17,680
Well, the use of a turnstile is you can cut down on your manpower

402
00:25:17,681 --> 00:25:19,680
of an entrance.

403
00:25:19,681 --> 00:25:22,680
Basically, you can have one guard per five or six turnstiles, so

404
00:25:22,681 --> 00:25:24,680
you can have crowd control.

405
00:25:24,681 --> 00:25:29,680
Modern turnstiles come in two sizes, waist-high and full

406
00:25:29,681 --> 00:25:30,680
height.

407
00:25:30,681 --> 00:25:33,680
Both make use of a locking ratchet that restricts entry

408
00:25:33,681 --> 00:25:36,680
to one at a time.

409
00:25:36,681 --> 00:25:40,680
For both styles, the arms are the most important parts.

410
00:25:40,681 --> 00:25:42,680
Basically, what we're doing is we're taking raw material,

411
00:25:42,681 --> 00:25:45,680
and we're cutting to size and forming.

412
00:25:45,681 --> 00:25:48,680
The Chicago and New York transit systems both use

413
00:25:48,681 --> 00:25:52,680
Boone-Edom turnstiles to regulate crowd flow.

414
00:25:52,681 --> 00:25:55,680
Because of the extremely abusive environment

415
00:25:55,681 --> 00:25:57,680
of the New York City subway system,

416
00:25:57,681 --> 00:26:00,280
These have proven to be some of the most durable,

417
00:26:00,380 --> 00:26:03,680
reliable, long-lasting turnstiles on the planet.

418
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:06,320
A larger cousin of the turnstile appeared

419
00:26:06,420 --> 00:26:08,420
in the late 19th century.

420
00:26:08,520 --> 00:26:11,920
Theophilus Van Cannell patented the revolving storm door

421
00:26:12,020 --> 00:26:14,940
in the U.S. in 1899.

422
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:17,840
Well, the revolving door was then and still is to this day

423
00:26:17,940 --> 00:26:20,940
the only type of door that provides an effective,

424
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:22,980
complete seal to the outside,

425
00:26:23,080 --> 00:26:25,380
regardless of how often it's being used.

426
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:27,140
The old saying with revolving doors is

427
00:26:27,141 --> 00:26:30,640
is the door is always open, always closed.

428
00:26:30,641 --> 00:26:32,940
Van Canel's invention counteracted something

429
00:26:32,941 --> 00:26:35,280
called the chimney effect.

430
00:26:35,281 --> 00:26:37,700
When a swinging or sliding door is opened,

431
00:26:37,701 --> 00:26:41,080
air is sucked in and upwards, like it would be up a chimney

432
00:26:41,081 --> 00:26:43,240
in your home.

433
00:26:43,241 --> 00:26:46,000
The doors create a series of pressurized pockets

434
00:26:46,001 --> 00:26:50,840
that allow people to enter an exit, but limit the airflow.

435
00:26:50,841 --> 00:26:54,580
A 2006 study at MIT found that revolving doors

436
00:26:54,581 --> 00:26:59,280
let only one-eighth the amount of heated or cooled air escape.

437
00:26:59,281 --> 00:27:02,280
While a revolving door costs on average more than the

438
00:27:02,281 --> 00:27:05,420
traditional swinging or sliding door that it would replace,

439
00:27:05,421 --> 00:27:08,420
it does pay for itself in an average three to five-year

440
00:27:08,421 --> 00:27:13,140
period due to the energy savings.

441
00:27:13,141 --> 00:27:15,940
Moo Needham constructs the skeleton of a revolving door

442
00:27:15,941 --> 00:27:18,440
out of raw aluminum.

443
00:27:18,441 --> 00:27:21,380
Computers control the drilling and the cutting,

444
00:27:21,381 --> 00:27:25,620
But some parts require a hands-on approach.

445
00:27:25,720 --> 00:27:27,980
As revolving doors are round, it's necessary for us

446
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:30,620
to take the straight pieces of aluminum

447
00:27:30,720 --> 00:27:32,220
and give them a curvature.

448
00:27:35,620 --> 00:27:39,040
One of the final steps is testing the brains of the door.

449
00:27:39,140 --> 00:27:43,240
We'll attach the cables to the control box,

450
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:45,980
which connects to this miniature revolving door.

451
00:27:46,080 --> 00:27:49,880
We'll then run it through a sequence of tests.

452
00:27:49,881 --> 00:27:52,560
Revolving doors are ideal for security buildings

453
00:27:52,561 --> 00:27:56,880
because their computers can sense and control all entries and exits.

454
00:27:57,580 --> 00:28:01,360
In this particular door, we have a camera mounted up in the ceiling

455
00:28:01,361 --> 00:28:06,940
which is scanning the compartment and looking for two persons to come through or one person.

456
00:28:07,640 --> 00:28:09,440
Please step into the door.

457
00:28:09,441 --> 00:28:13,240
The door rejects unauthorized entries by backing up and forcing them out.

458
00:28:13,480 --> 00:28:16,420
Unauthorized entry. Please exit the door.

459
00:28:16,421 --> 00:28:21,700
Whether they talk or not, revolving doors frighten some people.

460
00:28:22,900 --> 00:28:26,960
Since a tragic 1942 fire at Boston's Coconut Grove,

461
00:28:27,740 --> 00:28:29,340
it was incorrectly blamed on one.

462
00:28:30,160 --> 00:28:32,420
Revolving doors have employed new safety features.

463
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:35,300
If enough pressure is applied to the wings,

464
00:28:35,560 --> 00:28:38,100
they will bookfold into a straight passageway.

465
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:43,560
Bookfolding is when the revolving door wings are collapsed in a manner

466
00:28:43,561 --> 00:28:47,820
that you can exit the building in the event of an emergency or a fire.

467
00:28:48,780 --> 00:28:52,160
You can't get your arm caught in the new electronic revolving doors.

468
00:28:52,640 --> 00:28:55,860
This door includes what we call a safety rail bent wall switch,

469
00:28:56,080 --> 00:29:00,340
which, once compressed, will cause the door to stop and or back up if necessary.

470
00:29:00,920 --> 00:29:04,840
What would happen is that the door will compress your hand against the switch

471
00:29:04,841 --> 00:29:07,120
and then back up.

472
00:29:08,480 --> 00:29:11,460
Simple features make everyday entries and exits safer.

473
00:29:11,461 --> 00:29:14,260
But when the stakes are life and death,

474
00:29:14,360 --> 00:29:18,860
you need extraordinary measures.

475
00:29:18,960 --> 00:29:22,100
Lou Hewitt and Dee Horton of Corpus Christi, Texas,

476
00:29:22,200 --> 00:29:25,600
invented the automatic sliding door in 1954

477
00:29:25,700 --> 00:29:29,620
to combat hurricane winds that slammed ordinary doors shut.

478
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:34,720
Enter and Exit will return on Modern Marvels.

479
00:29:34,820 --> 00:29:38,120
We now return to Enter and Exit on Modern Marvels.

480
00:29:41,460 --> 00:29:44,460
Certain entries and exits must be absolutely secure,

481
00:29:45,080 --> 00:29:46,460
sometimes to keep people out,

482
00:29:47,220 --> 00:29:50,240
and occasionally to keep something in.

483
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,660
Take the biosafety laboratories

484
00:29:52,661 --> 00:29:55,520
at the Southwest Foundation for Biomedical Research.

485
00:29:56,440 --> 00:30:00,180
Three levels of biosafety mean three different kinds of entries and exits.

486
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:07,180
Entering a level two biosafety lab requires only moderate safeguards.

487
00:30:08,160 --> 00:30:11,180
It houses agents like HIV, herpes, and hepatitis,

488
00:30:11,460 --> 00:30:13,400
which do not transmit by air.

489
00:30:14,140 --> 00:30:18,340
Infection would only occur if pathogens were exposed to open cuts or mucous membranes.

490
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:24,440
A special airflow system called negative pressure provides the first line of defense.

491
00:30:25,160 --> 00:30:29,560
Exhaust fans with special filters keep pressure inside the labs lower than outside.

492
00:30:30,680 --> 00:30:33,600
Outside air naturally flows toward a lower pressure environment.

493
00:30:35,720 --> 00:30:40,500
Airborne agents and anything light are literally sucked backwards inside the lab.

494
00:30:41,460 --> 00:30:49,760
As we walk through the door, you'll feel a gust of air pass by you, and this is indicating

495
00:30:49,761 --> 00:30:52,580
that we have proper negative flow into the lab.

496
00:30:52,581 --> 00:30:58,520
Scientists also work underneath biosafety cabinets that provide an area of even higher

497
00:30:58,521 --> 00:31:01,520
negative pressure than the rest of the lab.

498
00:31:01,521 --> 00:31:05,660
So if, for instance, you're opening up a tube and it managed to splatter some of the virus

499
00:31:05,661 --> 00:31:10,580
into the space, it makes sure that the small droplet particles aren't pushed out of the

500
00:31:10,581 --> 00:31:13,580
workspace, it stays within the workspace.

501
00:31:15,580 --> 00:31:20,580
Level 3 labs see work with pathogens like smallpox, TB, and West Nile virus.

502
00:31:20,581 --> 00:31:27,580
Diseases that can cause severe illness and can be transmitted by air, but for which there is a treatment.

503
00:31:27,581 --> 00:31:35,580
Here, scientists wear positive pressure biosafety suits, the same kind health workers use in the field during outbreaks.

504
00:31:35,581 --> 00:31:39,580
Positive pressure suits have a strong outward airflow

505
00:31:39,581 --> 00:31:41,580
that literally pushes pathogens away.

506
00:31:43,580 --> 00:31:46,580
The respirator does is creates an area of positive pressure

507
00:31:46,581 --> 00:31:49,580
around your face, so therefore the agents are kept

508
00:31:49,581 --> 00:31:51,580
out of your breathing environment.

509
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:56,580
A level 4 lab also has a negative pressure environment,

510
00:31:56,581 --> 00:32:00,580
and scientists work underneath biosafety cabinets.

511
00:32:00,581 --> 00:32:04,580
But no biosafety lab requires greater protection than level 4.

512
00:32:04,581 --> 00:32:09,260
The agents here include some of the most dangerous airborne diseases on the planet.

513
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:12,820
Ebola, Lassa fever, and anthrax.

514
00:32:14,740 --> 00:32:19,400
Level 4 agents are those that are deadly viruses, can cause disease.

515
00:32:19,920 --> 00:32:23,540
There's no cure or vaccine for, and they can be transmitted in the laboratory.

516
00:32:25,200 --> 00:32:28,440
An escape might mean a catastrophic spread of deadly pathogens.

517
00:32:28,441 --> 00:32:37,440
To enter and exit the Level 4 lab, scientists must go through an airlock in full spacesuit-style gear.

518
00:32:37,441 --> 00:32:42,440
The military uses suits similar to these in chemical and biohazard cleanup operations.

519
00:32:42,441 --> 00:32:47,440
Before we enter the DSO-4, we do an integrity test from the suit,

520
00:32:47,441 --> 00:32:50,440
where we plug it into our air supply line, fill it up with air,

521
00:32:50,441 --> 00:32:53,440
and try to detect any leaks in the suit itself.

522
00:32:53,441 --> 00:32:54,141
itself.

523
00:32:54,142 --> 00:33:00,560
The Southwest Foundation for Biomedical Research has the only privately owned BSL-4 in

524
00:33:00,561 --> 00:33:01,751
North America.

525
00:33:01,752 --> 00:33:08,640
While Level 3 suits are open to the outside, the Level 4 suits are self-contained

526
00:33:08,641 --> 00:33:11,260
and they need a constant air supply.

527
00:33:11,261 --> 00:33:15,480
Scientists change air hoses as they move from prep area to

528
00:33:15,481 --> 00:33:18,315
airlock to various stations within the lab.

529
00:33:18,316 --> 00:33:22,140
Wearing the suit's a little cumbersome and a little noisy,

530
00:33:22,141 --> 00:33:25,800
but we can do anything we could do in a regular lab in this suit.

531
00:33:27,120 --> 00:33:32,720
To avoid fatigue, scientists only work an average of three to five hours before leaving the lab.

532
00:33:33,320 --> 00:33:35,900
It takes maybe 20 minutes to get out of the lab,

533
00:33:36,380 --> 00:33:40,780
so when you think you might need to go to the bathroom, you have to think 20 minutes in advance.

534
00:33:42,440 --> 00:33:46,440
Nothing exits the lab without disinfection, not even research.

535
00:33:47,480 --> 00:33:50,300
So any paper we generate can't exit the lab,

536
00:33:50,301 --> 00:33:52,860
So we send paper out using this fax machine here.

537
00:33:54,500 --> 00:33:58,020
For the scientists, leaving involves another level of protection,

538
00:33:58,400 --> 00:34:01,100
a stronger and longer disinfection shower.

539
00:34:02,220 --> 00:34:06,360
The major ingredient is remarkably common, household Lysol.

540
00:34:08,620 --> 00:34:13,700
The lab controls entry and exit so strictly, they also sterilize liquid waste.

541
00:34:16,020 --> 00:34:19,660
Liquids from the sink and shower travel to the basement of the SFBR.

542
00:34:20,300 --> 00:34:25,000
At that point, the waste would come into the piping that's behind me,

543
00:34:25,240 --> 00:34:28,520
come into the 500-gallon process tank to my right.

544
00:34:28,860 --> 00:34:31,580
It is then heated up to 250 degrees Fahrenheit.

545
00:34:32,120 --> 00:34:35,700
It's held at that temperature for two hours and then cooled back down.

546
00:34:36,160 --> 00:34:38,940
The entire process takes seven and a half hours.

547
00:34:39,320 --> 00:34:43,880
At that point, then, it is deemed suitable to be dumped into the city sanitary system.

548
00:34:47,440 --> 00:34:49,520
Even the air from the labs is cleaned.

549
00:34:50,300 --> 00:34:55,300
All of the air coming from the BSL-4 lab comes through this duct at the point

550
00:34:55,301 --> 00:35:00,300
then they pass through the HEPA filters, high efficiency particulate air filters.

551
00:35:00,301 --> 00:35:07,300
Any viruses, spores, things of that nature are trapped within the pleats of the filter themselves.

552
00:35:07,301 --> 00:35:12,300
They're captured there and the filter maintains them in the filter itself.

553
00:35:12,301 --> 00:35:15,300
Nothing but the clean air passes through.

554
00:35:15,301 --> 00:35:20,840
There's a saying at the SFBR, nothing gets out alive except the humans.

555
00:35:20,841 --> 00:35:29,700
At the Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, humans don't come out of the cash vault alive

556
00:35:29,701 --> 00:35:34,980
because they never go in. Ten years ago, Cleveland became one of the first banks in

557
00:35:34,981 --> 00:35:41,480
the Federal Reserve System with a vault that no person enters or exits.

558
00:35:41,481 --> 00:35:43,860
Until 1997, bank employees

559
00:35:43,861 --> 00:35:48,620
entered and exited through a giant portal, 10 feet wide,

560
00:35:49,160 --> 00:35:53,240
5 feet thick, and weighing in at a stunning 100 tons.

561
00:35:54,580 --> 00:35:58,400
This is still the largest single vault door opening.

562
00:36:00,040 --> 00:36:05,200
The door was made so large because it served as a deterrent

563
00:36:05,201 --> 00:36:10,220
to anybody giving the thought of trying to break into the vault.

564
00:36:10,221 --> 00:36:15,940
The door is balanced on a hinge that weighs 47 tons by itself.

565
00:36:16,840 --> 00:36:20,100
However, it's calibrated so two people can move it by hand.

566
00:36:21,220 --> 00:36:26,320
Its seal is so tight, a paper clip caught in the frame was permanently embedded.

567
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:32,480
Since 1997, humans don't go into this vault.

568
00:36:33,040 --> 00:36:36,320
The Federal Reserve decommissioned it in favor of a brand new one

569
00:36:36,321 --> 00:36:38,940
and a more efficient system of entering and exiting.

570
00:36:40,220 --> 00:36:43,820
Robots, or AGVs.

571
00:36:43,821 --> 00:36:47,660
An AGV is an automatic guided vehicle.

572
00:36:48,160 --> 00:36:51,600
Our tellers will simply just, by a couple of keystrokes,

573
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:57,580
request a container of currency or deposit a container of currency to the vault,

574
00:36:57,900 --> 00:36:59,420
and the system takes over.

575
00:36:59,920 --> 00:37:04,580
The AGV will pick up the container and deposit it in a P&D station,

576
00:37:04,760 --> 00:37:07,000
which is a delivery or pickup station.

577
00:37:07,001 --> 00:37:12,820
When it's deposited there, it communicates with the crane to pick up the container

578
00:37:12,821 --> 00:37:15,840
and then deposit the container into a racking system.

579
00:37:17,960 --> 00:37:23,040
The AGVs enter and exit through two gates that are monitored by security cameras.

580
00:37:26,400 --> 00:37:32,320
A sensor limits entry to the AGVs and scans to make sure their containers have not been tampered with.

581
00:37:32,321 --> 00:37:42,920
The sizing station reads the AGV, reads the barcode on the container, reads the barcode on the seal to make sure that it's all correct.

582
00:37:42,921 --> 00:37:53,935
And then it also sizes the size of the AGV and the container to ensure that no one else is trying to piggyback or sneak into the vault as the AGV is going into the

583
00:37:53,936 --> 00:37:54,636
vault.

584
00:37:55,620 --> 00:37:59,900
The AGVs are the only ones with access to tens of billions in cash.

585
00:37:59,901 --> 00:38:03,040
and each one can carry a lot of it at once.

586
00:38:04,040 --> 00:38:08,740
430,001 bills or 43 million in hundreds.

587
00:38:10,880 --> 00:38:14,680
The historic vault was built for image.

588
00:38:15,440 --> 00:38:16,860
This is built for efficiency.

589
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:23,040
The goal of some entries and exits

590
00:38:23,041 --> 00:38:25,100
is to provide absolute security.

591
00:38:25,760 --> 00:38:29,000
But sometimes a little trickery might do the job.

592
00:38:29,001 --> 00:38:30,620
When is a door not a door?

593
00:38:31,300 --> 00:38:32,280
When it's a secret.

594
00:38:36,900 --> 00:38:38,240
On an average day,

595
00:38:39,360 --> 00:38:42,440
7 million pieces of currency enter and exit the cash vault

596
00:38:42,441 --> 00:38:43,900
at the Cleveland Federal Reserve.

597
00:38:45,420 --> 00:38:48,020
Enter and exit will return on Modern Marvels.

598
00:38:50,260 --> 00:38:53,980
We now return to enter and exit on Modern Marvels.

599
00:38:55,860 --> 00:38:58,820
If your everyday doorways seem a little mundane,

600
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:03,320
There's one way to spice up an entry or exit, a hidden door.

601
00:39:05,460 --> 00:39:10,200
At Creative Home Engineering, bookcases and walls are really entries and exits.

602
00:39:11,140 --> 00:39:16,160
Some people say, oh, I want the book that people pull and then that opens the secret door.

603
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:21,500
That's kind of the stereotypical Scooby-Doo sort of opening, and it's a really fun one.

604
00:39:21,540 --> 00:39:22,600
So we do a lot of those.

605
00:39:22,940 --> 00:39:27,620
We'll make it so you move some chess pieces on a chess board to just the right spot.

606
00:39:29,000 --> 00:39:34,000
Secret doors may conceal vaults or panic rooms.

607
00:39:34,100 --> 00:39:36,500
Panic rooms are hiding places used in the event

608
00:39:36,600 --> 00:39:38,500
of home invasion.

609
00:39:38,600 --> 00:39:40,940
Some have hidden cameras that allow those who go in to see

610
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:43,640
if coming out is safe.

611
00:39:43,740 --> 00:39:46,360
Most of the secret doors that we have done in the past

612
00:39:46,460 --> 00:39:49,060
are a secret entrance into an area

613
00:39:49,160 --> 00:39:52,860
where there's some sort of safety.

614
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:55,500
Devotees of the secret door are themselves

615
00:39:55,501 --> 00:40:00,500
Devotees of the secret door are themselves a bit secretive.

616
00:40:00,501 --> 00:40:04,500
This door is going to one of the world's wealthiest people.

617
00:40:04,501 --> 00:40:07,500
I can't tell you, of course, who it's going to exactly.

618
00:40:07,501 --> 00:40:09,500
That's kind of typical for us.

619
00:40:09,501 --> 00:40:13,500
We do a lot of extremely wealthy clientele.

620
00:40:13,501 --> 00:40:16,500
Some hidden doors are just cosmetic.

621
00:40:16,501 --> 00:40:19,500
We're at the offices of an aviation company here,

622
00:40:19,501 --> 00:40:22,500
and they have a nice, new, modern office,

623
00:40:22,501 --> 00:40:25,540
but they had a very unsightly utility closet

624
00:40:25,541 --> 00:40:26,860
that they didn't want to look at anymore.

625
00:40:27,220 --> 00:40:31,240
So they had us come in and build this bookcase secret passageway,

626
00:40:31,640 --> 00:40:37,000
and we pull this airplane, and the door opens.

627
00:40:37,560 --> 00:40:39,160
You can see that's where they keep their mop.

628
00:40:40,240 --> 00:40:43,540
Hidden doors have to fit snugly so no one can tell what they are.

629
00:40:45,460 --> 00:40:50,800
So this client wanted their room to be covered in this nice wall paneling,

630
00:40:50,801 --> 00:40:57,400
And so we've taken all the trouble to make sure, you know, all the seams are perfect and it looks beautiful all the way.

631
00:40:57,740 --> 00:41:02,380
And you can see there's no seam, there's no gap, there's no light coming through.

632
00:41:02,700 --> 00:41:07,120
And when I tilt the book switch, you can see the secret door emerges.

633
00:41:11,120 --> 00:41:14,620
There's a lot of careful engineering hidden inside the hidden door.

634
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:20,340
So our secret doors have a metal skeleton that's hidden under all the carpentry.

635
00:41:20,341 --> 00:41:26,120
And it's very carefully engineered to be able to handle the overhung loads that are involved in a secret passageway.

636
00:41:27,460 --> 00:41:31,720
But creative home engineering has to know when to turn down a prospective client.

637
00:41:32,720 --> 00:41:38,820
I've had a couple of people say, I want a trap door so that when I pull a lever, someone would fall through it.

638
00:41:39,120 --> 00:41:45,840
Or I even had a guy once that wanted automated machine guns to pop out of his hallway.

639
00:41:45,841 --> 00:41:50,840
Hidden doors and passages have a rich history.

640
00:41:50,940 --> 00:41:53,640
In ancient Egypt, burial tombs employed them

641
00:41:53,740 --> 00:41:57,180
to protect treasure from grave robbers.

642
00:41:57,280 --> 00:42:00,080
Secret passages have been used in medieval times,

643
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:02,280
and there are castles throughout Europe

644
00:42:02,380 --> 00:42:05,500
that have secret passageways that were used for secret

645
00:42:05,600 --> 00:42:09,800
escapes for the king in case of an invasion.

646
00:42:09,900 --> 00:42:12,500
Tsar Paul I built a secret exit

647
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:15,040
in the Michalovsky Castle, but was

648
00:42:15,041 --> 00:42:18,180
but was assassinated in his bedroom before he could use it.

649
00:42:19,460 --> 00:42:22,740
Pope Alexander VI employed a secret passage called the Paseto

650
00:42:22,741 --> 00:42:25,220
to escape Vatican City during an invasion.

651
00:42:26,880 --> 00:42:29,060
Marie Antoinette used one in 1789

652
00:42:29,061 --> 00:42:32,960
to make her last successful escape from an angry mob.

653
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:39,480
In the 1920s, speakeasies employed secret doors

654
00:42:39,481 --> 00:42:42,420
to disguise their storefront, their liquor stock,

655
00:42:42,860 --> 00:42:44,380
and the backdoor escape route.

656
00:42:45,040 --> 00:42:47,300
And then if there was ever a raid,

657
00:42:47,720 --> 00:42:50,540
everyone would have time to escape out of the back door

658
00:42:50,541 --> 00:42:53,140
before the police came in and arrested everyone.

659
00:42:54,680 --> 00:42:57,200
Hidden or otherwise, we take for granted

660
00:42:57,201 --> 00:42:59,300
that our entries and exits lead somewhere.

661
00:43:00,300 --> 00:43:02,200
One woman planned just the opposite.

662
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:05,300
When Sarah Winchester designed her home,

663
00:43:06,340 --> 00:43:09,580
she instructed builders to put in windows, doors, and stairways

664
00:43:09,581 --> 00:43:12,420
that led nowhere.

665
00:43:13,180 --> 00:43:18,600
The home boasts 950 doors, and many of them go surprising places.

666
00:43:21,380 --> 00:43:27,400
Heiress to the Winchester rifle fortune, she believed herself haunted by the spirits of those the guns had killed.

667
00:43:28,340 --> 00:43:32,140
Her intent was to confuse and trap the ghosts with their strange designs.

668
00:43:32,900 --> 00:43:36,540
The project went on for 38 years until Sarah's death.

669
00:43:37,480 --> 00:43:41,540
The entries and exits we have are usually more functional than Sarah Winchester's.

670
00:43:42,420 --> 00:43:45,420
They come in all shapes and sizes.

671
00:43:45,421 --> 00:43:48,420
Unauthorized entry.

672
00:43:48,421 --> 00:43:52,420
But most importantly, we can't go anywhere without them.

